<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry, Count of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1066–1112</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Henry of Burgundy · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry, Count of Portugal, was a Burgundian noble who fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> of León against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. Around 1096 he married Alfonso's daughter Teresa and received the County of Portugal, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a>. He governed it until his death in 1112, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> became the first King of Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-count-of-portugal.jpg" alt="Henry, Count of Portugal" width="277" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry,_Count_of_Portugal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born in about 1066 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the youngest son of Henry, the second son of Robert I, Duke of Burgundy. His two older brothers, Hugh I and Odo I, inherited the duchy. No contemporary record of his mother survives. One of his paternal aunts was Constance of Burgundy, the wife of King Alfonso VI of León, and one of his great-uncles was Hugh, Abbot of Cluny.</p><p>After the defeat of the Christian troops at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Battle of Sagrajas</a> in October 1086, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> appealed for aid from Christians beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, and many French nobles and soldiers answered, among them Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Henry's brother Odo I and Raymond of St. Gilles. His presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> is confirmed only from 1096, when he appears confirming the forais of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a> and Constantim de Panoias.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Three of these French nobles married daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. Raymond of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> married Urraca, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urraca-of-leon-and-castile">Queen Urraca</a> of León, Raymond of St. Gilles married Elvira, and Henry married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-countess-of-portugal">Teresa of León</a>, an illegitimate daughter of the king by his mistress Jimena Muñoz. Between 1096 and 1105 Raymond, whose influence at court was diminishing, made a pact with his cousin Henry, the birth of Alfonso's only son Sancho Alfónsez having alarmed them both. They agreed to share power and the royal treasury. Raymond promised Henry the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and one third of the treasury on Alfonso's death, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> if he could not deliver Toledo.</p><p>Some historians date the pact near 1096 and think the king, hearing of it, made Henry governor of the lands between the Minho and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>. Others show that it cannot have been made before 1103. After Raymond's death Urraca married King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a>, and Henry took advantage of the family conflicts to serve on both sides and enlarge his domains. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a> under siege by the king of Aragon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry died on 22 May 1112 of wounds received during the siege. By his own orders his remains were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Braga</a> and buried in a chapel of Braga Cathedral, whose building he had promoted. His widow then ruled alone. He led a group of French gentlemen, monks and clerics who exerted great influence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, promoted reforms and introduced institutions from beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, such as the customs of Cluny and the Roman Rite. Their prominent positions provoked a strong backlash in the last years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>'s reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry, Count of Portugal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Henry of Burgundy</b>, a grandson of Duke Robert I, became count of Portugal, and his son Afonso Henriques became the first King of Portugal, founding the dynasty known as the Portuguese House of Burgundy, which ruled until 1383.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…to the Tagus and later the Guadiana laid foundations for the Crown of Castile, and the County of Portugal, granted by Alfonso VI to his son-in-law <b>Henry of Burgundy</b>, became the independent Kingdom of Portugal under Afonso Henriques, recognised by León in the Treaty of Zamora in 1143.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Portugal grew out of the County of Portugal, the land between the Minho and the Mondego that Alfonso VI of León granted in the 1090s to <b>Henry of Burgundy</b>, husband of his daughter Teresa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1096 Alfonso VI of León recreated it, now including Coimbra, for <b>Henry of Burgundy</b>, husband of his daughter Teresa, who ruled after Henry's death in 1112.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-portugal">County of Portugal</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Astorga, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1095" aria-label="Dijon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4668 504 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry, Count of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5118 774h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5118 774h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.32,5.04&amp;year=1095">What was here in 1095? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
